BLOCK|h1|1|Cluster-lang: Chapter 7 — Database & Table Queries
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang features a built-in lightweight SQL database engine (powered by SQLite) directly integrated into the language syntax and standard library.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|1. Initializing Databases
BLOCK|p||You can open or create an in-memory or file-based database instance:
BLOCK|code|python|// Opens a file-based SQLite database
db := DB("app.db")
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|2. Defining Tables
BLOCK|p||To declare a table schema, define a model and register it as a database table:
BLOCK|code|python|model Employee:
    id: int
    name: string
    salary: float

// Create table inside the database matching the Employee model layout
db.create_table[Employee]()
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|3. Querying & Manipulating Data
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang provides native methods for inserting, updating, and querying table records.
BLOCK|h3|3|Inserting Records
BLOCK|code|python|emp1 := box Employee(id=1, name="Alice", salary=75000.0)
db.insert(move(emp1))
BLOCK|h3|3|Querying Records (ORM-style)
BLOCK|p||Query records using clean filtration predicates:
BLOCK|code|python|// Retrieve employees earning more than 50,000
results := db.query[Employee]("salary > 50000")

for emp in results:
    put emp.name
    put emp.salary
BLOCK|h3|3|Direct SQL Execution
BLOCK|p||For complex queries, you can run raw SQL directly:
BLOCK|code|python|db.execute("UPDATE Employee SET salary = salary * 1.1 WHERE id = 1")
BLOCK|p||This native DB integration makes Cluster-lang an exceptional fit for building fast, self-contained, microservice backends without external library overhead.
BLOCK|hr||
